#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064009 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Linear Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "4d4edfc8-e178-58b4-b827-e4310ead130e" :question/math-academy-id "e-485" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 \\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $4$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1} = 4 -\\dfrac{5}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-\\frac{5}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{0}^{1}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[4-0]-5[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =4-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064010 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f110daa3-fd31-5e34-8a88-0e002c2e418a" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator is Linear"}] :question/id #uuid "74761123-d539-5d78-8dcd-4ea1db04bed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-486" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division. \n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{ 2x^2-3x-4}{x-3} = 2x + 3 + \\dfrac {5}{x-3} .\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064011 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d71bc2-c0f0-5504-9f79-a5f5cdb8cd92" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator Contains a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "cf016fa7-c3a2-5f9b-84a6-7b005d0d7286" :question/math-academy-id "e-487" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} & =1-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Linear Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aec6078-01a4-5b59-93d5-68b741df2d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314906} {:answer/id #uuid "49eaa986-5e8a-5ee8-bfcb-04d71b74d6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314907} {:answer/id #uuid "43207d35-a11f-5227-b363-5116699634ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314908} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7bc3a5-251e-5de7-9737-1b55ced5e7a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314909} {:answer/id #uuid "3e35a6e5-48f5-5ec7-92a6-fa0e877d97c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43207d35-a11f-5227-b363-5116699634ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314908} :answer-field/id #uuid "585def09-c78b-5de6-9b00-3dbc3c05e2dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8994c3eb-f396-5b4f-a637-dc6bb7953e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-49354" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5x+7}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 \\\\  & ↓ & -10 \\\\  & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+7}{x+2}=5-\\frac{3}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+7}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5-\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Linear Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f4887b-3d01-58b1-bcd9-d2fb19997842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314913} {:answer/id #uuid "2e147fd6-0f93-5c00-8018-d07ef10f0ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314914} {:answer/id #uuid "2708a6b9-fb75-5c92-9425-f57e1167c066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314915} {:answer/id #uuid "8f75d583-8a2e-5cf8-a698-0af2fdc387f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314916} {:answer/id #uuid "c248c35d-9a92-551e-802d-626ecaaa5da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2708a6b9-fb75-5c92-9425-f57e1167c066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314915} :answer-field/id #uuid "461040fd-6236-57ff-af4a-4babd622bde1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fef7e5a4-8743-520c-8404-5503ee85f90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49398" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{x+4}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\  & ↓ & -3 \\\\  & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+4}{x+3}=1+\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{x+4}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1+\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{|}_{-2}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-(-2))+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-2+3|) \\\\  & =2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f110daa3-fd31-5e34-8a88-0e002c2e418a" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator is Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b57d584-29e0-514d-8ce7-8bf95af333ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314934} {:answer/id #uuid "0dcd9336-343a-59a5-ae33-3e75a9e8f1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-3x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314935} {:answer/id #uuid "953549fb-6843-5e38-a909-0b2d32bb2e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314936} {:answer/id #uuid "66c47afb-0399-5e9b-bb15-85438050f36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314937} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0308f5-8d24-5bea-8cea-4b6d5f17f3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b57d584-29e0-514d-8ce7-8bf95af333ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314934} :answer-field/id #uuid "06777435-7464-543f-9a23-0ac5af9f8489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b477fe5-67ac-5170-be29-6f6d97baa23e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49456" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 3 & -9 & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & 9 & 0 & 3 \\\\  & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}=3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
